  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Biodiversity-friendly Economic Development through Cacao based Agroforestry Systems at La Amistad, Panama.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project will support over 500 Gnobe Bugle and Naso Teribe small-scale farmers, including 250 women, living in the lower ridges of the La Amistad Biosphere Reserve to convert 5,000 hectares of currently low-production cacao groves and abandoned fields within the Reserve to productive cacao-based agroforestry farms and prevent their conversion into pasture land. Project activities include: 1) provide technical assistance, including farmer exchanges, and provision of equipment to small-scale indigenous farmers, 2) establish communal cacao seedling nurseries with Frosty Pod Rot resistant varieties, 3) construct/rehabilitate three bulking and stories facilities to ease collection and transportation to markets, and 4) obtain organic certification of at least 1,000 hectares of cacao and banana based agro-forestry systems and link farmers to international markets and premium prices for their certified products.</narrative>
  </description>
